  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/11-gymnotiformes-knifefishes-of-the-new-world/adontosternarchus_clarkae_en/",
      "html": "<p>A small knifefish (maximum length up to 20 cm) from Venezuela. It is a very nice and peaceful species. Suggested common name: Marbled Knifefish.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "beforeYouBuy",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/11-gymnotiformes-knifefishes-of-the-new-world/adontosternarchus_clarkae_en/",
      "html": "<p>This species is only rarely imported, and only a few specimens could be obtained. Available size: 8–10 cm.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/11-gymnotiformes-knifefishes-of-the-new-world/adontosternarchus_clarkae_en/",
      "html": "<p>Venezuela.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/11-gymnotiformes-knifefishes-of-the-new-world/adontosternarchus_clarkae_en/",
      "html": "<p>Regarding water chemistry the fish seem to be undemanding. However, soft and acid water should be preferred over hard and alkaline water. The water temperature should be a little higher than usual, about 26–28°C.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "equipment",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/11-gymnotiformes-knifefishes-of-the-new-world/adontosternarchus_clarkae_en/",
      "html": "<p>Knifefishes are most active at dawn, so the tank should not be too brightly lit and should contain a good number of hiding places.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/11-gymnotiformes-knifefishes-of-the-new-world/adontosternarchus_clarkae_en/",
      "html": "<p>The fish feed basically on Tubifex and frozen fish food.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/11-gymnotiformes-knifefishes-of-the-new-world/adontosternarchus_clarkae_en/",
      "html": "<p>Like all South American knifefishes, this species is weakly electric and communicates with conspecifics via electrical impulses. These impulses are also used for orientation.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/11-gymnotiformes-knifefishes-of-the-new-world/adontosternarchus_clarkae_en/",
      "html": "<p>The genus Adontosternarchus currently comprises four accepted species, which can best be distinguished by differences in head profile combined with the shape of the mouth.</p><p>Adontosternarchus: ancient Greek, means \"Sternarchus without teeth\"; Sternarchus is another genus of knifefish. clarkae: dedication name for Ms. Kate Clark, who was the first collector of the species.</p>"
    }
  ],
